theory.intro:
The **angle of inclination** $\theta$ of a tangent is the angle it makes with the positive $x$-axis, measured anticlockwise, with $0^{\circ} \le \theta < 180^{\circ}$. Its gradient is $m = f'(x_1)$, and $m = \tan\theta$.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\usetikzlibrary{angles, quotes}
\draw[->] (-3.3,0) -- (2.8,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->] (0,-1.0) -- (0,3.4) node[above] {$y$};
\coordinate (V) at (-0.15,0);
\coordinate (Ax) at (1.5,0);
\coordinate (Bt) at (1.9,2.46);
\coordinate (P) at (1.2,1.62);
\draw[thick, <->, domain=-2.0:2.0, smooth, samples=60, variable=\x] plot ({\x}, {0.5*\x*\x + 0.9}) node[pos=0.1, left] {$y=f(x)$};
\draw[thick] (-0.5,-0.42) -- (Bt) node[pos=0, below left] {tangent};
\fill (P) circle (2pt);
\node[above left] at (P) {$P$};
\pic[draw, "$\theta$", angle radius=0.6cm, angle eccentricity=2] {angle = Ax--V--Bt};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

facts:
A negative gradient gives an obtuse angle, and $\theta = 180^{\circ} + \tan^{-1}(m)$, since the calculator returns a negative value.

